<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Voroneț Monastery</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1488">1488</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Voroneț is a monastery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukovina">Bukovina</a>, north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-the-great">Stephen the Great</a> in 1488. Its exterior walls were painted in 1547 under Metropolitan Grigore Roșca, and its Last Judgement and intense blue pigment, known as 'Voroneț blue', made it the best known of the painted churches of northern Moldavia, which form a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/voronet-monastery.jpg" alt="Voroneț Monastery" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dstefanescu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Voronet_Intrare.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The churches of Stephen the Great and his successors, and especially the sixteenth-century monasteries whose outer walls were covered with frescoes under Petru Rareș and later princes, including <b>Voroneț</b>, Humor, Moldovița, Arbore and Sucevița, are a group on the UNESCO World Heritage List.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
